ing of the  impact of the food you've consumed  on your health and performance and it  would also need to assess your lifestyle  your goals and a lot of this information  is information you may not want to be  providing to  different corporations or governments  rightly so but the author does rightly  remark that  we do already give up a lot of this  information every time we use the  internet i guess depending  on what browser you use such as what we  buy what we  read where we travel and our activity  levels  and another piece of information they  might also want  is your microbiome composition and so  there actually already is a company that  exists  that i've spoken about before in this  channel called fiom  and what filium claims to be able to do  is to take  information from your microbiome and to  give you  a personalized nutrition however like  all of these different  companies at the moment the different  algorithms or  machine learning techniques that they  use to make these nutrition profiles  initially needs to be trained on data  and so  over time over increased amounts of data  the assessments and outputs of these  different  algorithms will improve and so i guess  it just depends  well it just needs information lots and  lots  and lots and lots of information  and as i said as well at the idea about  potentially needing  continuous measurements doing blood  samples  or microbiome assessments isn't really  feasible  on a daily schedule so what you would  really need is personal biometric  sensors  and i guess the ultimate goal would be  to measure biomarkers without  having to collect and analyze biological  samples such as wearable devices  and it may not be too far into the  future until we see such devices  because some of them already exist for  example there are now  wearable devices that can measure blood  glucose levels continuously and  painlessly  which has been especially useful for  diabetic patients  and so the author's take is that there's