ing of the  impact of the food you've consumed  on your health and performance and it  would also need to assess your lifestyle  your goals and a lot of this information  is information you may not want to be  providing to  different corporations or governments  rightly so but the author does rightly  remark that  we do already give up a lot of this  information every time we use the  internet i guess depending  on what browser you use such as what we  buy what we  read where we travel and our activity  levels  and another piece of information they  might also want  is your microbiome composition and so  there actually already is a company that  exists  that i've spoken about before in this  channel called fiom  and what filium claims to be able to do  is to take  information from your microbiome and to  give you  a personalized nutrition however like  all of these different  companies at the moment the different  algorithms or  machine learning techniques that they  use to make these nutrition profiles  initially needs to be trained on data  and so  over time over increased amounts of data  the assessments and outputs of these  different  algorithms will improve and so i guess  it just depends  well it just needs information lots and  lots  and lots and lots of information  and as i said as well at the idea about  potentially needing  continuous measurements doing blood  samples  or microbiome assessments isn't really  feasible  on a daily schedule so what you would  really need is personal biometric  sensors  and i guess the ultimate goal would be  to measure biomarkers without  having to collect and analyze biological  samples such as wearable devices  and it may not be too far into the  future until we see such devices  because some of them already exist for  example there are now  wearable devices that can measure blood  glucose levels continuously and  painlessly  which has been especially useful for  diabetic patients  and so the author's take is that there's  enormous potential for personalized  nutrition  if improvements in state-of-the-art  computational approaches  to collect the biometric data can be  achieved as well as the system's biology  to bring together the knowledge from  different sources  and to create a more detailed picture of  their relationship between diet and  health  in individuals and so if we go back now  to one of the  two questions that i introduced at the  beginning what should i  in my family eat i think it's pretty  clear that the answer to this question  is very complicated  and will be specific to individuals  so in the second half of the book david  addresses the second question  how will we feed the growing world  population and  the best way i thought to kind of go  through this section is to break it down  into three parts the first looking at  food biotechnology  then we'll look at food nanotechnology  and lastly we'll talk about  replacements of traditional animal  products with more sustainable and  ethical alternatives  so starting with foods by technology  david makes it very clear that he  understands that people have differing  opinions  over the use of food biotechnology such  as genetically modified crops  and he raises the point that we need to  just be able to listen to each other and  to understand the science  and the book just tries to address the  science that goes  behind all of these different  applications that said the risks and  benefits of these different technologies  should always be assessed so beginning  with genetically modified crops  the common examples are genetic  modification such that you can enhance  the growth of the crops and  improve its resistance to different  diseases and so the idea being that  the crop yield is greater and so this  could help create more food for the  growing population  but genetic modification can also be  used for bio-fortification  for example genes of stable crops can be  altered so  plants naturally retain more iron and  iron deficiency is currently the most  common form of micronutrient deficiency  in the world  so these applications have health  considerations as well as  sustainability applications and it can  also be used to eliminate harmful  substances  for example it has been used to create a  new type of wheat with much lower levels  of gluten  gluten can cause an autoimmune response  in some people  resulting in the condition celiac  disease so by improving yields  resilience and versatility  food biotechnology has continuous  potential for the future  however david does reiterate that it is  impossible to say  that all genetically engineered foods  are completely safe  but it is also wrong to say that just  because a food has been genetically  modified  that it is unsafe and highly respected  scientific organizations  actually strongly support the use of  genetic engineering in foods and  agriculture  based on the fast body of literature  including the world health organization  so i knew quite a bit about food  biotechnology before reading this book  but what i didn't know so much about was  food nanotechnology  which is what david specializes in so  this phrase may sound a bit scary but  nanotechnology refers to creating and  utilizing structures with minuscule  dimensions  so it's just working with things that  are very small and the  benefits of doing this is that very  small structures  have unique properties such as one  their small size obviously but second by  having such a small size they have a  much  larger surface area to volume ratio and  that can be exploited  and lastly they have high reactivity due  to the fact that the properties of  matter  inside particles change when they become  very small due to quantum effects  but don't worry we won't go into that  because i don't really understand it so  how will food  nanotechnology help with solving the  solution  for increased food demand well david  gives a couple of examples in the book  such as feeding plants with nano  fertilizers  and protecting crops with nanopesticides  but the coolest example  in my opinion are sensors made from  carbon nanotubes that can be absorbed by  plants  and can actually send signals to a  smartphone when a crop becomes  contaminated with pesticides  and so this comes from research in a  nature paper that came out  in 2016 which you can see here and it  goes into more detail about how the  system would actually work  but the idea is that only a couple of  the plants would need to  have their sensor and they could be  indicators for the whole crop  and so it wouldn't necessarily be that  you're consuming  these carbon nanotubes along with your  salads i don't know  anyway david highlights that there are  three main hurdles  before we could see nanotechnology  becoming widespread  in foods firstly food nanotechnology  would need to become  economically viable and become more cost  effective than competing technologies  secondly there'd have to be no health or  environmental risks with using these  different nano technologies  such as these nano fertilizers and then  lastly  and probably the most important it would  have to be accepted by the public  and so lastly we'll look at how  traditional animal products can be  replaced with more sustainable and  ethical alternatives  and so the reason that doing this  achieves the second question  of how will we feed the growing world  population  comes from the quite alarming statistics  that  whilst meat consumption only makes up  around 15  of the energy requirements of the global  population  it uses around 80 percent of the  available  agricultural land for either grazing or  producing fodder  however it should be stated that the  environmental impact does differ  depending on the kind of meat  with beef being more damaging to the  environments than  pork poultry or fish production however  it should be stated first that  the meat industry has become efficient  in using  every part of the animal including the  skin bones  and hooves to create other products such  as converting it to gelatin  which is used in the creation of yogurts  for example  also david also mentions that in the  future there will definitely still be a  place for animals  partly because animals can live on land  that isn't suitable for arable  production  and they can also convert grass leaves  husks shells  into high quality sources of protein  vitamins and minerals  so what alternatives are there well it's  very likely that you've either seen in  shops  and potentially even tried yourself  plant-based alternatives david addresses  plant-based alternatives by looking at  plant-based burgers  for example you have plant-based burgers  produced by impossible foods and also  beyond meat  but to get anywhere close to actually  mimicking the  meat's burgers you need to understand  the physics and chemistry of meat  structure  including that of the muscle fibers the  connective tissue  and the adipose tissue in whole meads  and also how  those different structures and  components change during the cooking  process  and one example is the fact that meat  goes from this reddish color  when it's uncooked to brown when it is  cooked  and that's due to the oxygen being  strongly bound to a protein known as  myoglobin  and when that temperature change occurs  during cooking  there's a structural transformation of  this protein myoglobin  such that oxygen is released and there's  an iron k factor in this molecule as  well  that loses an electron and actually  transitions the collar from red to brown  so how could that be mimicked using  plant-based alternatives  well impossible foods identified a  plant-based alternative to myoglobin  leg hemoglobin and it comes from the  root nodules of soybeans  and so this can give the plant burger a  red meat like appearance  and also the meaty flavor so there's  definitely a lot of research going into  plant-based alternatives at the moment  but this isn't the only solution another  way that you can actually consume meat  in a more ethical manner comes from  cultured meat  and so that's where beef pork chicken or  fish can be grown in a lab or a factory  from living cells  taken from animals without having to  kill them the way this is done can be  described through  three steps first of all you have the  collection stage  and so this is where cells are taken  from the living animal  and so these cells can be stored and  used later on  and it's been estimated that only 150  cows  could in theory produce all the beef the  world currently needs  once you have these cells the second  stage is the proliferation stage which  kind of given by its name is when  the cells grow and they replicate and  then lastly you have the maturation  stage  so here the conditions of the  environment  in which the cells are growing is  altered so you get the desired  cell type for example if you're wanting  to make beef you'd want them to be  generating  muscle adipose and connective tissue  cells  so as a scientist who has much  experience with growing cells in a lab i  honestly think  this is really ingenious and really  awesome and so if you're also interested  you should check out  a video on one company that is  developing cultured meat  i believe it's just pronounced just and  you can watch a video of them  generate chicken nuggets from a chicken  that they called  ian and they eat these chicken nuggets  whilst ian is like just walking about  besides them so that's kind of cute  but the interesting application of this  is that  you can grow meat from just like one or  two cells from a live  animal so what if we took one of our own  cells  yeah it's a little bit grim kind of  freaks me out when i read it too  i mean i definitely would only ever  consider eating meat from my own cells  i don't know but the point is that  it's possible and continuing with  neophobia  fear of new foods and disgust and other  possible  future foods or what is already  considered a food just not so much in  western culture  is the consumption of insects or  entomophagy  to be precise so the ethical benefits of  eating insects is the fact that  most edible insects don't have a central  nervous system  so don't feel pain and in terms of the  sustainability  insects are more efficient at converting  feed into food  and leads to fewer greenhouse gases than  animals and they can eat waste such as  wasted food crops  to grow and there's also the potential  to eat insects that would otherwise be  pests  for other plants but if eating bugs  still creeps you out  then there's also the potential for the  insect to be dried and ground into  flowers  however what might concern you is that  we're already entomophagists  the fda apparently allows a certain  number of insect parts and certain food  products  for example 20 maggots per 100 grams of  mushrooms  35 fruit fly eggs per 230 grams of  golden raisins  and 475 insect fragments per 50 grams of  ground pepper  yum anyway along with these other  technologies eating insects  the safety considerations will also need  to be taken into account  for example they may have toxins it may  change our microbiome  however so far the european food safety  authority  have reviewed evidence and said that  there's no more risk consuming insects  over other forms of food as long as  they're handled and processed correctly  to ensure their safety  so maybe we'll be seeing insects a lot  more in the future  and so the last alternative that david  mentions  are microbial meat and you most likely  have heard about this  for example from quorn the mycoprotein  produced by the fungus fusarium  phenonatum  and along with insects and plant-based  alternatives  the advantages to using microbial  species  is that they reduce food waste they  obviously use  much less land and produce much less  pollution  and an exciting advance mentioned by  david  is from a finnish company referred to as  solar foods  and they claim to make food out of thin  air  effectively they're coaxing autotrophic  bacteria into producing  a nutrient-rich edible protein using  only air and sunlight and a few minerals  according to their presentation their  novel protein  solene requires 10 times less land than  conventional agriculture  a hundred times less greenhouse gases  are produced  than meads and it doesn't cause  pollution because it uses a closed  system  the challenges they face will be scaling  up and using their product and  integrating it into  different food products that people will  actually want to eat  but a really cool use of this technology  would be  if we ever go to mars or the fast  astronauts to go to mars  in terms of being able to generate good  food source  bacteria that can generate a protein  from minimal  inputs would be really desirable so to  put all of this information together  david concludes that he's partially  convinced  that science and technology must play a  critical role in ensuring a healthier  and more sustainable food supply  and the best tk message creating word  for words  the future of our food supply is too  important for us to make decisions based  on irrational beliefs  rather than sound evidence and the point  is we need to become more informed  to listen engage with others who have  differing opinions  and advocate for the future we believe  in through our actions  purchases and foods so hopefully this  video has given you a good very brief  probably  not that in-depth overview to this book  but i hope it gives you some  insight into what we could see in the  future and  how important science is to these  different applications  and generally i think yeah the book was  really interesting and  got me excited and fascinated at the  same time it's always a good thing  so go check out the book there's a lot  of things i didn't  get to mention so yeah  so i hope you've learned something and  as always thanks for listening
 

